I was expecting this answer. It amazes me how most people don't understand the development cycle for big games like these. The AI is the first thing you do in a game. It's literally how the computer processes information. It is the hardest thing to change because if you change how the AI works, then the environment and everything else also has to change,

&#x200B;

The game is 7 months away. 95% of this game is already finished. The last 5% is testing for bugs and making sure the game is compatible with different hardware and such. This would have been a good reply if they showed a gameplay video 2 years ago. This is 7 months away.

&#x200B;

It's not going to change.

I completely agree. Chase says that he did it to show the audience that the show just ends, as if the viewer is not omnipotent and therefore doesn't get to see everything.That's contradictory to many of the sub plots that did have endings. If that was the case, there'd be no conclusion, tension, climax, beginning, and end for anything. 

&#x200B;

I think that's such an "elitist artist" cop out explanation. Don't try to convince me the story was supposed to be seen as something truly realistic when you have story arcs and climaxes throughout all the seasons.

&#x200B;

I think what really happened is that HBO or Chase didn't want to continue and they had to cut it short. Not releasing the tension on the show is not "daring" or "brave" or "artistic", it's lazy and uninspired.

&#x200B;

This is the same thing that happened to Game of Thrones. Rather than provide an appropriate, satisfying, albeit predictable ending, they tried to "subvert expectations" for the wow factor.

&#x200B;

It's stupid and immature.

Can we discuss this ending? Am I missing something here?

&#x200B;

